  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 333 AM CDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nOn Tuesday, weak southerly low level flow is expected to resume as a \ncold front slowly approaches from the north, driven by a more \nsubstantial trough digging into the Great Lakes. While we don't \nexpect strong warm-air advection and moisture return, this should be \nenough for a modest northward rebound of warmer temperatures Tuesday \nafternoon, although with the modified airmass still in place, we \ndon't expect that this will be enough to reach Advisory level heat \nlocally. Instead, expect temperatures near or slightly above \naverage, the latter generally across the Ozarks, and dry conditions \nduring the day. While we don't expect storms to develop during the \nday, there has been a persistent signal for showers/thunderstorms to \ndevelop along the advancing front across Iowa, which then advance \nsouth southeastward into our area later in the evening and \novernight. This may pose at least a limited threat for strong or \nsevere thunderstorms Tuesday night/Wednesday morning, with a \npreference for our northern areas given the late arrival of this \nactivity, although this will depend on how thunderstorms evolve as \nthey move further and further south after dark. \n\nChances for showers and thunderstorms also continue into Wednesday \nand possibly even Thursday, although much will depend on how quickly \nthe passing cold front surges south and out of the area. Medium \nrange guidance does hint at additional shortwaves moving through the \narea during this time frame, and if the boundary is on the slower \nside, it wouldn't be out of the question to see additional rounds of \nshowers and thunderstorms late Wednesday into Thursday, more likely \nthan not as elevated storms as the low level jet overrides a slowly \nadvancing or or stalled surface front. Meanwhile, confidence \ncontinues to increase that even after a modest temperature rebound \nTuesday, this reinforcing front will drive milder temperatures back \ninto the area Wednesday through the end of the week.",
